[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `fgets` does not read across newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `char in[100]` with `fgets(in, sizeof(in), stdin)` reads **at most 99 bytes**,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stops after the first `\n` (which it keeps), and never consumes a later line.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust does `stdin.lock().take(99).read_until(b'\n', &mut buf)`, which has the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m same three stopping conditions.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The 99-byte boundary is where an off-by-one would hide, so it is tested from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m both sides: 98 chars + `\n` (exactly 99 bytes, newline included), 99 chars +
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `\n` (newline *not* read), 100 digits, `"1" * 120` (truncated to 99 digits,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m which then becomes `ERANGE` → error path), `"1"` followed by 105 `'0'`s, a `'-'`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sitting on the 99th byte, `INT_MAX` ending exactly at the boundary, and 200
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m spaces before the digits (the digits fall outside the window, so `strtol`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m performs no conversion → error path).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m See `fgets_99_byte_boundary`, `fgets_does_not_read_across_newlines`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. `fgets` failure leaves the buffer as `""`, not indeterminate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `in` is initialized with `= ""`, so it is 100 zero bytes. When glibc `fgets`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m returns `NULL` it does not write to the buffer, so `in` is still the empty
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m string; `strtol("")` performs no conversion, `endp == str`, and the program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m prints `An error occurred` and **still returns 0**. The Rust ignores the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `read_until` error and leaves `buf` empty, producing the same output and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m same exit status. Verified with `/dev/null` (EOF) and with a directory on stdin
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (EISDIR).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. Embedded NUL bytes truncate the string
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fgets` copies a NUL from the stream like any other byte, but `strtol` then
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m treats it as the end of the string. The Rust truncates the buffer at the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m NUL after reading. Tested with a leading NUL (→ error path), a NUL between
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m digits, a NUL after a complete number, and a NUL after the sign.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m See `embedded_nul_bytes_terminate_the_c_string`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. `strtol` accepts leading whitespace, a sign, and trailing garbage
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `parse_val` checks only `endp != str`, so `"12abc"`, `"0x10"` (base 10 stops at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `x`), `"1e5"`, `"4.9"`, `"5\r"` from a CRLF line, and `"  42"` are all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **accepted**, yielding 12, 0, 1, 4, 5, 42. Conversely `"+"`, `"-"`, `"--5"`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `" - 5"`, `".5"` and `"_5"` perform no conversion and reach the error path. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust `strtol_base10` reproduces the C-locale whitespace set (space, `\t`, `\n`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `\v`, `\f`, `\r`), the optional single sign, and the digit run.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m See `successful_conversions_with_strtol_quirks`, `no_conversion_performed`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. `ERANGE` vs. the `int` range check are two different rejections
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Values that overflow `long` set `errno = ERANGE` and fail the `errno == 0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m check; values that fit in `long` but not `int` pass that check and fail
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tmp >= INT_MIN && tmp <= INT_MAX`. Both end at the same `printf`, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m distinction is invisible in the output — but it is a real pair of branches and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m both are exercised, along with `LONG_MAX`, `LONG_MAX + 1`, `LONG_MIN`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `LONG_MIN - 1`, `INT_MAX ± 1` and `INT_MIN ± 1`. The Rust clamps at `i64` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reports an `erange` flag that gates `errno_is_zero`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m See `long_range_and_erange`, `int_range_boundaries`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 6. Signed overflow of `bedrooms`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `house->bedrooms += extra_bedrooms` is signed-integer overflow, i.e. UB in C,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `run` is called **twice on the same house**, so with `x = INT_MAX` bedrooms
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m goes `5 → -2147483644 → 3`. The Rust uses `wrapping_add`, which matches what
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m gcc emits here — confirmed with the C compiled both without optimization flags
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and at `-O3`. `add_floor` likewise uses `wrapping_add`, though `floors` only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ever reaches 4.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m See `bedroom_accumulation_overflow`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 7. State shared across the two `run` calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `run(&the_house, x)` is called twice on the same struct, so the second call
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m starts from the mutated values: floors `2→3→4`, bathrooms `2.5→3.5→4.5`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bedrooms accumulating `x` twice. All eight `print_house` lines are pinned in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `both_binaries_run_and_c_output_is_the_documented_reference`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 8. `%.1f` formatting
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `bathrooms` only ever takes 2.5, 3.5 and 4.5 — exactly representable in binary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and already at one decimal place — so `printf("%.1f")` and Rust's `{:.1}` cannot
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disagree on rounding, and the program never calls `setlocale`, so the decimal
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m separator is `.` in both. No tie-breaking or locale difference is reachable.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 9. stderr and exit status
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Neither program writes to stderr on any input, and both always exit 0 —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m including on the error path, since `main` ends in a single `return 0`. A test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m asserts this explicitly rather than relying on the diff, because a stdout-only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m comparison would let an exit-status regression through.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m See `stderr_always_empty_and_exit_status_always_zero`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
